## Migration to .NET 8: Cooperative Cancellation

### Pattern Classification

Your code uses **three** Thread.Abort patterns:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `while (_running)` | `CancellationToken` |
| ThreadAbortException as control flow | `catch` block with `RollbackCurrentTransaction()` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| Thread.ResetAbort to continue | Keeps loop alive after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` and decide |
| Blocking call interruption | `GetNextItem()` may block | Pass token to blocking call |

### Why This Breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work. The runtime no longer forcibly injects exceptions into threads. You **must** switch to cooperative cancellation.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just let the loop condition exit naturally
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive after timeout, there's nothing more we can do
        // cooperatively — log a warning in production code
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

### Key Changes to `GetNextItem`

The blocking `GetNextItem()` **must** be made cancellation-aware, otherwise cancellation stalls until it unblocks on its own:

```csharp
// BEFORE: blocks indefinitely, only Thread.Abort could break it
private WorkItem GetNextItem() 
{
    return _queue.Take(); // blocks forever
}

// AFTER: unblocks when token is cancelled
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}
```

If you're using `BlockingCollection<T>`, it already accepts a `CancellationToken`. If you're using a custom blocking mechanism, replace `WaitOne()` with:

```csharp
WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _itemAvailable, token.WaitHandle });
token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why It Matters |
|---------|----------------|
| **Not passing token to `GetNextItem`** | The thread stays blocked forever — cancellation never takes effect. This is the #1 migration mistake. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers and registered callbacks. Always `Dispose()`. |
| **Using `Thread.Sleep` for the timeout in `Stop`** | Use `Thread.Join(timeout)` instead — it actually waits for thread completion. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid catching unrelated OCEs from nested operations. |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only fires at the next checkpoint. If `ProcessItem` is long-running, add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside it at regular intervals. |

### Fundamental Paradigm Shift

`.NET Framework`: The runtime **forcibly injects** `ThreadAbortException` anywhere in your thread — even inside `finally` blocks.

`.NET 8+`: Your code **must voluntarily check** for cancellation. If you never check `token.IsCancellationRequested` or call a token-aware API, the thread runs forever regardless of `Cancel()`.
